# Paths never synced between the laptop and the cluster.
# Used by both directions:  rsync --exclude-from=.rsync-exclude
#
# Two reasons a path lands here: it is huge and already present on both sides,
# or copying it would actively break the destination.

# --- generated data: huge, and each side already has it -------------------
# data/ is 17 GB (300 shards, 10.8M paths). It is generator output, not source;
# regenerating is deterministic from the per-env seeds.
data/
data1/

# --- environments: copying these BREAKS the destination -------------------
# A venv hardcodes absolute interpreter paths in every console-script shebang
# and in pyvenv.cfg, and its compiled extension modules are built for the local
# Python ABI, CPU and CUDA. Synced to a machine with a different Python, it is
# not merely useless -- it can shadow the working interpreter and fail in
# confusing ways. Install from requirements-train.txt on each machine instead.
.venv/
venv/

# --- history: larger than the working tree --------------------------------
# 7.2 GB here vs ~1.2 MB of actual source. Use git remotes, not rsync, for this.
.git/

# --- build artefacts: stale copies can win over fresh source --------------
# rsync preserves mtimes, so a synced .pyc can appear newer than the .py it was
# built from and get used instead. Let each machine compile its own.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.pytest_cache/
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# --- run outputs: direction-specific, so never in a blanket sync ----------
# These flow cluster -> laptop only, and are pulled explicitly by name.
# Leaving them here stops a code push from clobbering results on the cluster.
checkpoints/
results/
logs/
wandb/
*.png
